WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.449
Welcome to Module 3 of The Zero
Trust Assessment as Evidence:

2
00:00:04.449 --> 00:00:07.000
from results to a closure register.

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.525
In this module the guide's results
kit does the mechanical part.

4
00:00:10.525 --> 00:00:14.845
It normalizes every result, lists what
didn't run with what to do about it,

5
00:00:14.845 --> 00:00:19.238
and writes a closure-register entry
for each finding, routed and weighed.

6
00:00:19.238 --> 00:00:22.075
People do the rest, and
the register shows where.

7
00:00:22.075 --> 00:00:27.090
It covers Steps 7 and 8 of the Zero
Trust Assessment implementation guide,

8
00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:31.000
and takes about fifty-five
minutes, most of it in Lab 1.

9
00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:35.900
Why does this matter?

10
00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:39.338
A report that says seventeen
failed can't be acted on.

11
00:00:39.338 --> 00:00:43.873
Someone has to decide which of those
are real findings, who owns each one,

12
00:00:43.873 --> 00:00:47.563
which page in the workbook
remediates it, what comes first,

13
00:00:47.563 --> 00:00:50.176
and which are accepted
for now, and by whom.

14
00:00:50.176 --> 00:00:52.588
The results kit does the mechanical part.

15
00:00:52.588 --> 00:00:54.750
It normalizes every result.

16
00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:57.612
It lists what didn't run,
with what to do about it.

17
00:00:57.612 --> 00:01:01.281
And it writes one closure-register
entry for each finding,

18
00:01:01.281 --> 00:01:03.038
routed to a page and weighed.

19
00:01:03.038 --> 00:01:07.256
People do the rest: the owner fills
their entries, writes why a finding

20
00:01:07.256 --> 00:01:11.400
is weighted as it is, and someone
with the authority decides closure.

21
00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:15.010
The register shows exactly
where each of those lands.

22
00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:21.673
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module.

23
00:01:21.673 --> 00:01:24.699
You'll normalize a Zero
Trust Assessment export

24
00:01:24.699 --> 00:01:27.347
and read its counts by
result and by pillar.

25
00:01:27.347 --> 00:01:31.884
You'll work the dispositions for results
that are neither a pass nor a finding.

26
00:01:31.884 --> 00:01:34.847
You'll record the review
of an investigate result.

27
00:01:34.847 --> 00:01:38.252
You'll read a closure-register
entry and say what's filled,

28
00:01:38.252 --> 00:01:40.860
what a person fills,
and who decides closure.

29
00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:44.057
And you'll route a finding to
its family's remediation page,

30
00:01:44.057 --> 00:01:45.922
and check an exception's approver.

31
00:01:45.922 --> 00:01:50.672
You need Module 1, and Module 2's
Lab 2 for this module's Lab 2.

32
00:01:50.672 --> 00:01:56.039
In Unit 2 you'll read two chapters of Zero
Trust and Assessment: Close Before You

33
00:01:56.039 --> 00:02:01.920
Assess, for the finding families, and
Evidence, Not Verdicts, for the closure chain.

34
00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:08.232
Unit 1 normalizes and disposes.

35
00:02:08.232 --> 00:02:13.157
The kit writes each result twice: the
tool's status and the normalized one.

36
00:02:13.157 --> 00:02:18.207
It never copies the tool's result text,
because that text can name tenant objects.

37
00:02:18.207 --> 00:02:21.508
Everything that is neither
a pass nor a finding lands

38
00:02:21.508 --> 00:02:24.358
in a dispositions file,
with what to do about it.

39
00:02:24.358 --> 00:02:27.483
A skip for licensing needs
a licensing decision.

40
00:02:27.483 --> 00:02:32.065
A skip because a service was
disconnected, or a result the kit is

41
00:02:32.065 --> 00:02:36.295
unable to evaluate, needs a fix
and a re-run, or a check by hand.

42
00:02:36.295 --> 00:02:39.252
A skip as not applicable
needs a recorded reason,

43
00:02:39.252 --> 00:02:42.357
and not supported is flagged
as a boundary finding.

44
00:02:42.357 --> 00:02:43.395
One more guard.

45
00:02:43.395 --> 00:02:48.213
If the export comes from a version the
kit's taxonomy wasn't reviewed for,

46
00:02:48.213 --> 00:02:52.133
or carries a status or reason it
doesn't know, the kit says so.

47
00:02:52.133 --> 00:02:56.850
It counts that result as unable to
evaluate, rather than guessing.

48
00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:02.988
Unit 2 opens the closure register.

49
00:03:02.988 --> 00:03:08.324
Every finding gets one entry, with the
fields of the closure chain from Evidence,

50
00:03:08.324 --> 00:03:13.095
Not Verdicts: the finding and the tool
version, the attack path, the owner,

51
00:03:13.095 --> 00:03:18.269
the dependencies, the change, the pilot,
the enforcement, the test, the evidence,

52
00:03:18.269 --> 00:03:23.363
the residual risk, the reassessment,
the weighting, and the closure decision.

53
00:03:23.363 --> 00:03:26.691
The kit fills what it knows from
the tool and the family table,

54
00:03:26.691 --> 00:03:29.487
and leaves placeholders
for what a person decides.

55
00:03:29.487 --> 00:03:32.126
And it never marks a
finding ready for closure.

56
00:03:32.126 --> 00:03:36.565
The authorizing official decides that
for a plan of action and milestones item,

57
00:03:36.565 --> 00:03:38.987
and the assessor for
an assessment finding.

58
00:03:38.987 --> 00:03:41.410
The register records who decided.

59
00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:47.709
Each test I D routes to a finding family,

60
00:03:47.709 --> 00:03:51.772
and each family to the page in
the workbook that remediates it.

61
00:03:51.772 --> 00:03:54.249
The table is from Close Before You Assess,

62
00:03:54.249 --> 00:03:58.547
with the evidence the guide adds for
when a finding is ready for closure.

63
00:03:58.547 --> 00:04:01.760
Emergency access goes to
Access and Privilege,

64
00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:05.548
and is ready when the sign-in
alert fired on a test sign-in.

65
00:04:05.548 --> 00:04:09.352
Standing privilege goes to its
PIM chapter, and is ready when no

66
00:04:09.352 --> 00:04:13.448
permanent active assignment sits
outside the exception register.

67
00:04:13.448 --> 00:04:16.766
Device compliance goes to
Devices and Endpoints,

68
00:04:16.766 --> 00:04:20.085
ready when a noncompliant
test device is refused.

69
00:04:20.085 --> 00:04:24.023
Application secrets go to
Governance, Drift and Evidence.

70
00:04:24.023 --> 00:04:26.798
DDoS and web application firewall findings

71
00:04:26.798 --> 00:04:29.498
go to the Azure Security
Engineering guide.

72
00:04:29.498 --> 00:04:34.523
A finding no family covers is unrouted,
and goes to the Zero Trust program lead.

73
00:04:34.523 --> 00:04:38.668
In release three point one point
oh, the email and collaboration

74
00:04:38.668 --> 00:04:44.040
threat-protection tests have no family
yet, so they stay unrouted and visible.

75
00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:51.465
Two more parts of Unit 2:
weighing and exceptions.

76
00:04:51.465 --> 00:04:53.947
The kit sets a default priority:

77
00:04:53.947 --> 00:04:58.053
severity divided by effort,
in buckets P one to P four.

78
00:04:58.053 --> 00:05:01.364
That's design guidance, in
the kit's taxonomy file.

79
00:05:01.364 --> 00:05:06.732
Then the owner weighs each finding on
six factors from Evidence, Not Verdicts:

80
00:05:06.732 --> 00:05:09.500
exploitability, blast radius, exposure,

81
00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:13.190
compensating controls,
effort and dependency order.

82
00:05:13.190 --> 00:05:15.978
The owner writes the
rationale into the entry.

83
00:05:15.978 --> 00:05:19.166
The register also lists
each family's dependencies,

84
00:05:19.166 --> 00:05:23.540
so a Conditional Access finding waits
for emergency access to be right.

85
00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:27.078
Exceptions follow the same
rule as ScubaGear's omissions.

86
00:05:27.078 --> 00:05:30.552
An exception with an expiry
is the see-so's decision.

87
00:05:30.552 --> 00:05:33.739
One without an expiry is a
standing risk acceptance,

88
00:05:33.739 --> 00:05:36.777
and only the authorizing
official can approve it.

89
00:05:36.777 --> 00:05:40.633
The kit checks each one, and
marks it a governed exception,

90
00:05:40.633 --> 00:05:43.700
a lapsed acceptance, or a wrong approver.

91
00:05:47.450 --> 00:05:48.175
Try it.

92
00:05:48.175 --> 00:05:51.513
Lab 1 normalizes and
registers two fictional

93
00:05:51.513 --> 00:05:54.412
runs of a Contoso tenant, two weeks apart.

94
00:05:54.412 --> 00:05:56.888
Step one normalizes the first run.

95
00:05:56.888 --> 00:06:02.281
Thirty-three tests: eight pass, seventeen
fail, one planned, two skipped for

96
00:06:02.281 --> 00:06:07.763
licensing, one disconnected, two not
applicable, and two unable to evaluate.

97
00:06:07.763 --> 00:06:10.588
Seventeen findings, one
of them investigate.

98
00:06:10.588 --> 00:06:15.513
The tool marked sixteen failed; the
investigate result makes seventeen.

99
00:06:15.513 --> 00:06:19.513
The eight that are neither pass nor
finding are in their own columns.

100
00:06:19.513 --> 00:06:21.862
Step two reads the dispositions.

101
00:06:21.862 --> 00:06:26.962
Two skips for licensing, two not
applicable, one disconnected, one planned.

102
00:06:26.962 --> 00:06:32.166
And two unable to evaluate: test two
four five four eight ended in error,

103
00:06:32.166 --> 00:06:34.499
and four one oh five oh timed out.

104
00:06:34.499 --> 00:06:38.062
Both of those are a run to
fix, not a control to fail.

105
00:06:38.062 --> 00:06:40.622
The slide leaves out
the written-to line and

106
00:06:40.622 --> 00:06:43.400
the table's underline;
the page shows them.

107
00:06:47.150 --> 00:06:50.734
Step three writes the
register for the first run:

108
00:06:50.734 --> 00:06:55.125
seventeen findings, three in P one,
ten in P two, four in P three.

109
00:06:55.125 --> 00:06:59.075
And a warning: test four one
oh three two is unrouted.

110
00:06:59.075 --> 00:07:02.456
That's the Safe Links test,
which has no finding family,

111
00:07:02.456 --> 00:07:04.300
so the program lead routes it.

112
00:07:04.300 --> 00:07:06.850
Step four reads the top five rows.

113
00:07:06.850 --> 00:07:10.600
Two device compliance findings,
owned by endpoint engineering.

114
00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:15.288
A DDoS and web application firewall
finding, owned by the platform team.

115
00:07:15.288 --> 00:07:17.403
The unrouted Safe Links finding,

116
00:07:17.403 --> 00:07:20.850
owned by the Zero Trust
program lead until it's routed.

117
00:07:20.850 --> 00:07:22.912
And an application secrets finding.

118
00:07:22.912 --> 00:07:25.350
Look at two one eight three seven.

119
00:07:25.350 --> 00:07:29.844
It's the investigate result, and its
severity is weighted down, so it ranks

120
00:07:29.844 --> 00:07:34.563
below other high-risk findings of the
same effort until a person confirms it.

121
00:07:34.563 --> 00:07:38.900
Every finding has an owner, even
the one nobody has claimed yet.

122
00:07:42.650 --> 00:07:46.200
Steps five and six take the
second run, two weeks later.

123
00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:51.557
The reviews file holds one row: endpoint
engineering confirmed the investigate

124
00:07:51.557 --> 00:07:56.275
result, two one eight three seven, on
November eleventh, with a note on why.

125
00:07:56.275 --> 00:07:59.585
The kit normalizes the
second run with that review:

126
00:07:59.585 --> 00:08:01.950
thirty-four tests, fifteen findings.

127
00:08:01.950 --> 00:08:04.400
The exceptions file holds two rows.

128
00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:09.322
The first, on two five five three
three, was approved by the see-so,

129
00:08:09.322 --> 00:08:14.150
with an expiry at the end of
January, 2027: a governed exception.

130
00:08:14.150 --> 00:08:17.305
The second, on two one eight eight five,

131
00:08:17.305 --> 00:08:20.663
was approved by the I S
S O, with no end date.

132
00:08:20.663 --> 00:08:22.675
The kit marks it a wrong approver.

133
00:08:22.675 --> 00:08:26.289
An exception with no end date
is a standing risk acceptance,

134
00:08:26.289 --> 00:08:29.313
and only the authorizing
official can approve it.

135
00:08:29.313 --> 00:08:33.590
So the register warns, and that
exception goes back to be fixed.

136
00:08:37.340 --> 00:08:41.569
Lab 2 runs the same steps on the
export from Module 2's Lab 2,

137
00:08:41.569 --> 00:08:43.278
on the assessment host.

138
00:08:43.278 --> 00:08:44.839
The authors haven't run it.

139
00:08:44.839 --> 00:08:49.321
You replace the report folder with Module
2's, and the reviews and exceptions

140
00:08:49.321 --> 00:08:54.015
files with your own copies of the kit's
two templates, filled for your test tenant.

141
00:08:54.015 --> 00:08:58.190
Step one normalizes the export
and lists the dispositions.

142
00:08:58.190 --> 00:09:01.465
Step two writes the register,
with your exceptions.

143
00:09:01.465 --> 00:09:05.865
Step three is a comparison by eye:
open the report's pillar tabs.

144
00:09:05.865 --> 00:09:09.601
Every failed and investigate test
should have a register entry,

145
00:09:09.601 --> 00:09:11.152
and no skipped test should.

146
00:09:11.152 --> 00:09:14.102
Note any unrouted test
for your program lead.

147
00:09:14.102 --> 00:09:15.602
Step four backs it out.

148
00:09:15.602 --> 00:09:18.322
The outputs hold your
test tenant's results,

149
00:09:18.322 --> 00:09:21.100
so delete them when you finish Module 4.

150
00:09:24.850 --> 00:09:27.373
The federal requirement, in NIST special

151
00:09:27.373 --> 00:09:30.712
publication eight hundred
fifty-three, revision five.

152
00:09:30.712 --> 00:09:36.712
C A two, C A seven and C M six: results
normalized and counted honestly.

153
00:09:36.712 --> 00:09:39.391
The evidence is the normalized results,

154
00:09:39.391 --> 00:09:42.800
the summary, the dispositions,
and the reviews file.

155
00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:48.928
C A five, the plan of action and milestones:
each finding's planned remediation,

156
00:09:48.928 --> 00:09:52.375
evidenced by the register,
in jay-son and C S V.

157
00:09:52.375 --> 00:09:56.739
And R A seven, risk response: findings
weighed against risk tolerance,

158
00:09:56.739 --> 00:09:59.725
and exceptions approved
by the right official.

159
00:09:59.725 --> 00:10:03.450
The evidence is the weighting
rationale and the exceptions file.

160
00:10:03.450 --> 00:10:07.584
For C A five and R A seven, no
key security indicator is listed:

161
00:10:07.584 --> 00:10:12.225
the mapping in Governance, Drift
and Evidence pairs none with them.

162
00:10:12.225 --> 00:10:16.390
The evidence supports the
controls; the assessor decides.

163
00:10:20.140 --> 00:10:22.115
Check yourself with two questions.

164
00:10:22.115 --> 00:10:25.628
First: who marks a register
entry ready for closure?

165
00:10:25.628 --> 00:10:31.054
The answer is c: the authorizing official,
for a plan of action and milestones item,

166
00:10:31.054 --> 00:10:33.889
or the assessor, for
an assessment finding.

167
00:10:33.889 --> 00:10:37.290
The kit never does, and the
register records who decided.

168
00:10:37.290 --> 00:10:41.915
Second: an exception has no expiry,
and was approved by the see-so.

169
00:10:41.915 --> 00:10:43.302
What does the kit report?

170
00:10:43.302 --> 00:10:45.927
The answer is b, a wrong approver.

171
00:10:45.927 --> 00:10:48.758
With no expiry it's a
standing risk acceptance,

172
00:10:48.758 --> 00:10:51.664
and that's the authorizing
official's decision.

173
00:10:51.664 --> 00:10:54.283
The module page has three more questions,

174
00:10:54.283 --> 00:10:57.810
each linked to the unit or
lab step that answers it.

175
00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:02.385
To sum up.

176
00:11:02.385 --> 00:11:05.847
You normalized two runs and
worked their dispositions.

177
00:11:05.847 --> 00:11:08.823
You recorded the review
of an investigate result.

178
00:11:08.823 --> 00:11:13.846
You opened a closure register, routed to
the pages that remediate each family,

179
00:11:13.846 --> 00:11:16.672
with a default weighting
the owner refines.

180
00:11:16.672 --> 00:11:19.259
And you checked exceptions
against their approvers.

181
00:11:19.259 --> 00:11:24.840
The evidence an I S S O keeps from this
module: the normalized results and summary,

182
00:11:24.840 --> 00:11:29.660
the dispositions, the reviews and
exceptions files, and the register.

183
00:11:29.660 --> 00:11:31.585
Keep the out folder from Lab 1.

184
00:11:31.585 --> 00:11:35.313
Module 4, reconcile, re-run
and keep the evidence,

185
00:11:35.313 --> 00:11:38.420
compares these two runs, and seals them.
